window.HELP_TOC = {"groups":[{"title":"Get started","pages":[{"file":"index.html","title":"Welcome"},{"file":"workspace.html","title":"The Atelier window"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","title":"Keyboard shortcuts"}]},{"title":"Library","pages":[{"file":"import.html","title":"Import photos"},{"file":"library.html","title":"Organize and find"},{"file":"cull.html","title":"Cull: pick the keepers"},{"file":"photos.html","title":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing"}]},{"title":"Develop","pages":[{"file":"develop.html","title":"Develop basics"},{"file":"basic.html","title":"Basic"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","title":"Tone Curve"},{"file":"hsl.html","title":"HSL / Color and B&W"},{"file":"color-grading.html","title":"Color Grading"},{"file":"detail.html","title":"Detail"},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","title":"Lens Corrections"},{"file":"transform.html","title":"Transform"},{"file":"effects.html","title":"Effects"}]},{"title":"Tools","pages":[{"file":"crop.html","title":"Crop & Straighten"},{"file":"masking.html","title":"Masking"},{"file":"presets.html","title":"Presets"},{"file":"match-look.html","title":"Match Look"},{"file":"ai.html","title":"AI tools"}]},{"title":"Share","pages":[{"file":"export.html","title":"Export"},{"file":"edit-in.html","title":"Edit In other apps"},{"file":"hdr.html","title":"Single-image HDR"}]},{"title":"Set up","pages":[{"file":"settings.html","title":"Settings"}]}],"sections":[{"file":"index.html","id":"","title":"Welcome","page":"Get started","text":"Atelier catalogs your photos and edits them without ever changing the originals. Search with the field on the left, or ⌘F."},{"file":"index.html","id":"workflow","title":"From card to website in four steps","page":"Welcome","text":"Import a shoot: ⇧⌘I, or drag a folder onto the window. Cull to pick the keepers from each burst, then rate and flag with single keys (1–5, P, X). Develop (D): start with Basic, then crop, mask and grade. Export (⇧⌘E) with the Website quick setting."},{"file":"index.html","id":"develop-tools","title":"Develop panels","page":"Welcome","text":"BasicWhite balance, exposure, contrast, presence. Tone CurveShape brightness point by point. HSL / Color and B&WChange one color; mix black and white. Color GradingTint shadows, midtones and highlights. DetailSharpening and noise reduction. Lens CorrectionsDistortion, fringes, dark corners. TransformStraighten leaning verticals. EffectsVignette and film grain."},{"file":"index.html","id":"tools","title":"Tools","page":"Welcome","text":"Crop & StraightenTrim, level, rotate, flip. MaskingGradients, brush, subject: edit part of a photo. PresetsLooks in one click, and your own. Match LookBorrow the look of another picture. AI toolsRemove objects, replace backgrounds, upscale. ExportJPEG for the web, TIFF for print."},{"file":"index.html","id":"library-tools","title":"Library","page":"Welcome","text":"The Atelier windowA tour of every part. Import photosFrom a card, drive or the NAS. CullKeep the best of each burst. Organize and findRate, flag, filter, collect. About the pictures. Before/after pictures are made from the splash photos to show what each control does; they approximate Atelier's render. The control drawings match the app's Graphite theme."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"","title":"The Atelier window","page":"Get started","text":"One window with three views: Grid to browse, Loupe to look closely, Develop to edit. The parts around the photos stay in the same places in every view. ImportExport GridLoupeDevelopOrganize ⌄▯▮ LibraryAll Photographs1,284 ImportsRedwoods42Eagles, Homer310Watercolors18 VolumesMacintosh HDmegham offline CollectionsWebsitePrints 2026 Search filename, title, caption, keywordFilters ⌄ ⚑ ★★★★▤ 2 ★★★⧉ Copy 1 ★★RAW+JPG ⚑◫ 3 ★ ★★★✦ AI ✕ 42 photos · 1 selectedImported 42 photos Photo FileNZ6_9144.NEFCameraNIKON Z 6Lens24-70mm f/4 Rating and labelsRating★★★★FlagPicked Keywordsredwoods, light Redwoods · 42 photos · 1 selected · 1 of 42 · NZ6_9144.NEF ⌄ Import and Export. See Import photos and Export. Grid, Loupe, Develop: switch views (G E D). Organize adds the selection to collections and imports. Show or hide the Inspector. Sidebar: all photos, your imports, drives and folders, collections. In Develop it becomes presets, snapshots and history. Filter bar: search and filter by rating, flag and label. Grid, with badges under each photo. See Grid badges. Status bar: counts, and progress with Cancel for imports, exports and other background work. Inspector: the photo's details, rating, keywords. In Develop it becomes the editing panels. Drag its left edge to resize. Filmstrip: the current list, in every view. F6 hides it."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"views","title":"The three views","page":"The Atelier window","text":"Grid GBrowse, select, rate and organize. See Organize and find. Loupe EOne photo, large. Double-click to zoom to 100%; arrow keys move through photos. Develop DEdit the photo. See Develop basics."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"right-click","title":"Right-click everything","page":"The Atelier window","text":"Photos (in the grid, loupe, Develop and the Filmstrip), sidebar entries, panel headers, masks, presets and color wheels all have right-click menus with the commands that apply to them. Every command is also in a menu in the menu bar."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"themes","title":"Themes","page":"The Atelier window","text":"Atelier › Settings… › Appearance picks the look: System, Light, Dark, Graphite (like Lightroom), Darkroom (near black) or Neutral Gray (for judging color)."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"session","title":"Atelier remembers where you were","page":"The Atelier window","text":"When you open Atelier, it comes back to the view, sidebar item, filters, selection and zoom you left, with the same photo open in Develop. If that photo is gone or its drive is off, you'll see the grid with a note in the status bar."},{"file":"workspace.html","id":"catalog-recovery","title":"If the catalog cannot open","page":"The Atelier window","text":"⚠ Couldn’t Open Catalog ~/Pictures/Atelier/Atelier Catalog.atelier The reason appears here. Show in FinderQuitTry Again ↵ If Atelier cannot open its catalog, this screen replaces the splash. It names the location and reason. Try Again retries the same location after you resolve the problem, such as unavailable storage or access permissions. Show in Finder reveals the catalog, or its nearest available parent folder. Quit closes Atelier without making a catalog backup for the failed launch. Atelier does not delete, replace or reset the catalog, or automatically restore a backup. A catalog folder missing its database but containing previews, backups or other files is reported as a problem instead of opening an empty library. An empty folder, or one containing only Finder’s .DS_Store file, can finish a first launch that was interrupted. Use Show in Finder to locate the catalog’s Backups folder, where saved database copies are kept. Preserve a copy of the entire catalog package, including any database companion files, before attempting recovery. There is no built-in restore command yet. Library commands and settings become available once the catalog opens. Help › Atelier Help remains available while you resolve the problem. Next: Import photos · Keyboard shortcuts"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"","title":"Keyboard shortcuts","page":"Get started","text":"Single keys work as in Lightroom. They act in the library window, never while you're typing. Change them in Settings › Keyboard; Help › Keyboard Shortcuts (⌘/) shows your current keys."},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"single-keys","title":"Single keys","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","text":"Views GGrid ELoupe DDevelop ZZoom: Fit and 100% F6Show or hide the Filmstrip Rating, flags and labels 1–5 · 0Rate 1 to 5 stars · clear P · X · UPicked · Rejected · no flag 6 7 8 9Red, Yellow, Green, Blue label Develop \\Show before (toggle) YBefore and after: off, side by side, slider RCrop & Straighten X (while cropping)Switch landscape and portrait VBlack & White (toggle) WWhite Balance Selector JShow clipping on the photo (toggle) Masking ⇧WOpen or close Masking M · ⇧MLinear · Radial Gradient KBrush OShow Overlay [ ] · ⇧[ ⇧]Brush size · feather ⌥ while paintingErase DeleteDelete the selected part Return · EscDone · cancel the tool or close Transform ⇧TGuided Upright: draw guides O (in Guided)Show Loupe DeleteDelete the selected guide EscClose Guided Upright Cull P · X · SpaceKeep · drop · switch ArrowsMove between photos and groups Return · EscFinish · cancel"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"menu-shortcuts","title":"Menu shortcuts","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","text":"Library ⇧⌘IImport Photos… ⇧⌘EExport… ⌘S · ⌥⌘SWrite XMP for selection · all unsaved ⌘RRefresh Volumes DeleteRemove Photo… ⌘G ⌘E ⌘DGrid, Loupe, Develop Photo ⌘'Create Virtual Copy ⇧⌘GShow Cull Group ⌥⌘EEdit in (first app) ⇧⌘MMatch Look to Reference… Develop ⌘Z · ⇧⌘ZUndo · Redo ⇧⌘C · ⇧⌘VCopy · Paste Settings ⇧⌘RReset All Settings ⇧⌘UAuto White Balance ⌘[ · ⌘]Rotate left · right ⇧⌘NNew Preset… View and app ⌘= · ⌘-Zoom in · out ⌘0 · ⌥⌘1Zoom to Fit · 100% ⌘,Settings ⌘?Atelier Help (this window) ⌘/Keyboard Shortcuts"},{"file":"shortcuts.html","id":"mouse","title":"Mouse and trackpad","page":"Keyboard shortcuts","text":"Double-click the photoZoom to 100% and back at that spot Pinch, or ⌥/⌘ + scrollZoom freely Drag, scroll, Space + dragMove around when zoomed Double-click a slider's nameReset it ⇧ double-click a Tone slider's nameAuto for that slider ⌥ drag a sliderSee what it affects (⌥-drag views)"},{"file":"import.html","id":"","title":"Import photos","page":"Library","text":"Bring a shoot into Atelier from a card, a drive or the NAS. Leave the files where they are, or copy them into your library folder."},{"file":"import.html","id":"open","title":"Start an import","page":"Import photos","text":"Any of these opens the Import dialog: File › Import Photos… (⇧⌘I) or the Import button in the toolbar. Drag folders or photos from Finder onto the Atelier window. Right-click an import in the sidebar › Import More Photos Into This…"},{"file":"import.html","id":"dialog","title":"The Import dialog","page":"Import photos","text":"Import Photos ●Choose Photos— ○ Cull—○ Import 📁 100NZ6 128 ⊗ + Add… IMPORT INTO New import100NZ6 FILE HANDLING Leave files where they areCopy into my library folder APPLY DURING IMPORT None CULLING Cull before importing 128 photos · 5.9 GB · copied into Atelier Library · into “100NZ6” CancelImport 128 Photos Steps: where you are. Choose Photos › Import, or Choose Photos › Cull › Import with culling on. The dialog keeps the same size on every step. Sources: each folder or file you chose, with how many photos it has; ⊗ takes it out, Add… adds another. You can also drop folders or photos anywhere on the dialog. Photos: a thumbnail of every photo found (a RAW and its JPEG once). Import Into: a new import or an existing one. A new one is named after the first folder; the name is real text, so you can add to it or change it. File Handling: Leave files where they are (on this Mac or the NAS; nothing is moved) or Copy into my library folder, in dated folders, each copy checked against the original. Apply During Import: a develop preset and keywords for every photo. Culling: Cull before importing groups similar shots so you pick the keepers first. See Cull. What will happen, and the button, which says how many photos will be imported and stays dimmed until there's at least one. The panels on the right open and close like Develop's and stay as you leave them."},{"file":"import.html","id":"progress","title":"While it imports","page":"Import photos","text":"✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ ✓ 137 of 412 photos copied NZ6_9144.NEF COPIED6.1 of 18.4 GB SPEED180 MB/s TIME LEFTAbout 1 minute Each copy is checked against the original. Thumbnails are made once every photo is in.Run in Background Cancel Import The photos being imported, faint and grey until each one is in, then in full color with a check. The wall moves on by itself to keep the photos being copied in view. After culling it shows the photos you culled. How many are copied (or added, when files stay where they are), a progress bar and the file being copied. Copied, Speed and Time left. Run in Background and Cancel Import. The dialog stays open and shows how far it has got: how many photos are copied (or added) in large figures, a progress bar, the file being copied, and how much is copied, the speed and the time left. Cancel Import (Esc) stops it; photos already copied stay in the catalog. Run in Background closes the dialog and keeps the progress, with Cancel, in the status bar. When every file is in, the dialog closes, the new import is selected in the sidebar and its thumbnails are made. When it's done the status bar sums up what was imported, what was already in the catalog, and any files it couldn't read; Show Problems lists those with the reason. With Cull before importing on, Continue to Cull moves the same dialog to the Cull step. Back returns to your settings as you left them. Copied while you cull. As soon as the Cull step has listed the photos, Atelier starts copying them to your"},{"file":"import.html","id":"raw-jpeg","title":"RAW+JPEG pairs","page":"Import photos","text":"If your camera saves a RAW and a JPEG of each shot, Atelier shows them as one photo, the RAW, marked RAW+JPG. Ratings, flags, labels and keywords apply to both; editing and export use the RAW. Turn this off in Settings › Import › Show RAW+JPEG pairs as one photo."},{"file":"import.html","id":"defaults","title":"Defaults","page":"Import photos","text":"Atelier › Settings… › Import sets whether new imports leave or copy files, and where the library folder is. The dialog remembers your last choices. Next: Cull: pick the keepers · Organize and find"},{"file":"library.html","id":"","title":"Organize and find","page":"Library","text":"Browse in the grid, look closely in the loupe, rate and flag with single keys, and find any photo with the filter bar. SidebarGrid and LoupeRate, flag, labelThumbnail badgesFilter and searchInspectorCollections and importsFilmstripXMP sidecarsDrives and the NAS"},{"file":"library.html","id":"sidebar","title":"Sidebar","page":"Organize and find","text":"Library › All PhotographsEvery photo in the catalog. ImportsOne entry per import, named after its folder. Right-click to Rename…, Import More Photos Into This…, Cull… or Delete Import…. Deleting the list keeps the photos in the catalog and on disk, unless you tick Also delete the photos and move their files to the Trash. That box always starts unticked, and ticking it shows how many files will go to the Trash before you confirm with Delete Import and Files. VolumesYour drives and the NAS, each with its folders as they are on disk. An offline drive is marked; its photos stay browsable. CollectionsYour own lists. A photo can be in any number of them. + makes a new one."},{"file":"library.html","id":"grid-loupe","title":"Grid and Loupe","page":"Organize and find","text":"G · E · DGrid · Loupe (one photo, large) · Develop. Also the toolbar buttons and ⌘G ⌘E ⌘D. Double-click a thumbnail, or ReturnOpen it in Loupe. Return or Esc goes back to the grid. ← →Previous or next photo. Click · ⌘-click · ⇧-clickSelect one · add or remove one · select a run. Double-click in Loupe, or ZZoom to 100% and back. Drag to move around. Right-click a photoThe photo menu: open, edit, rate, flag, label, develop settings, presets, collections, Edit In, AI, Export, Remove Photo…"},{"file":"library.html","id":"rate","title":"Rate, flag, label","page":"Organize and find","text":"Single keys, as in Lightroom. They act on the selected photos (change them in Settings › Keyboard). Rating 1 … 5★ to ★★★★★ 0Clear the rating Flags PPicked ⚑ XRejected ✕ (dimmed in the grid) URemove the flag Color labels 6 7 8 9● Red · ● Yellow · ● Green · ● Blue"},{"file":"library.html","id":"badges","title":"Thumbnail badges","page":"Organize and find","text":"Thumbnails in the grid and the Filmstrip carry small badges in their corners, as in Lightroom's grid: Top left: 2 ⚑ ✕The photo's number in the Filmstrip, and its flag: picked, or rejected (the photo is also dimmed). Top right: ▤ 3An original with versions stacked on it (edits, AI results, virtual copies): 3 photos in all. See Stacks. ◪ Edit ✦ AI ⧉ Copy 1An Edit In version, an AI result, a virtual copy. RAW+JPGA RAW shown with the JPEG shot alongside it, as one photo. ◫ 4Culled with 3 other shots. ⇧⌘G shows the group (see Cull). Bottom left: ★★★★ ●The rating and the color label. Bottom rightEdited in Develop (sliders icon), cropped or straightened (crop icon), has masks (dashed circle), and ⚠ the original's drive is offline. Small thumbnails show names as icons and stars as “★ 4”. At the Filmstrip's smallest height only the flag, the label, the rating and one mark (offline first) remain. Hover over a thumbnail to read its file name and what its badges mean. The photo you're working on has a solid accent ring with a thin white line inside; the other selected photos have a lighter ring. This is the same in the grid, the Filmstrip and Cull, in every theme."},{"file":"library.html","id":"filter","title":"Filter and search","page":"Organize and find","text":"The bar above the photos narrows the current list: Search: type part of a filename, title, caption or keyword. Filters: a minimum Rating (★ + to ★★★★★), a Flag (Picked, Unflagged, Rejected), a Label and Edits (Edited or Unedited in Develop). The button shows how many are on. In the grid, the − + slider at the right of the bar makes thumbnails smaller or larger. Filters apply to whatever is selected in the sidebar, and to the Filmstrip. The Filmstrip's Filters button sets the same rules."},{"file":"library.html","id":"inspector","title":"Inspector","page":"Organize and find","text":"The right panel in Grid and Loupe (toggle it with the Inspector toolbar button). Its sections open and close like Develop's panels: click a header, or right-click one for Solo Mode, Expand All and Collapse All. Long names end in “…”; hover to read them in full. From the top: Histogram of the photo as edited, with clipping triangles, and under it the ISO, focal length, aperture and shutter speed it was taken with. Hover over that line for the camera, lens and exposure compensation. Editing it is in Develop. File: name, Paired With (the JPEG shot with a RAW, shown as one photo), copy name, folder, drive, dimensions and file size. Camera: capture time, camera, lens, exposure, ISO, focal length, exposure bias, flash, exposure program and metering mode. Rating, flag and label, Keywords (comma separated; Apply Keywords adds them to every selected photo), and Title and caption. In Loupe the photo itself also shows its name, when it was taken, its size and its camera settings in the corner, as Lightroom's Loupe info does."},{"file":"library.html","id":"collections","title":"Collections and imports","page":"Organize and find","text":"The Organize toolbar menu works on the selected photos: New Collection with Selection…, Add to a collection, Add to Import, and, when you're in a collection or import, Remove from This Collection or Remove from This Import. Removing from a list never removes the photo from the catalog."},{"file":"library.html","id":"filmstrip","title":"Filmstrip","page":"Organize and find","text":"The strip of thumbnails along the bottom, in every view, showing the current list in the same order as the grid. Its header, from the left: Source: the list's name and how many photos it shows. Click it to switch to All Photographs, one of the recent imports, a collection, or a folder (under its drive) without leaving the view you're in. How many photos are selected, which one you're on (“2 of 12”) and its file name. Filters: the same Flag, Rating, Color Label and Edits rules as the bar above the photos. When any are on, the button turns blue and lists them (“Picked · ★3+”); the ⓧ next to it clears them. Sort: Capture Time, Import Order, File Name, Rating or Edit Time. The arrow button next to it switches between ascending and descending. Versions stacked on a photo always stay right after it. The grid uses the same order, and Atelier remembers it. Click, ⌘-click and ⇧-click select, as in the grid. Right-click for the photo menu. Drag its top edge to make thumbnails bigger or smaller. Clicking a photo that is already in view never scrolls the strip; it scrolls only to bring the next photo in from the edge. Hide or show it with the small tab in the middle of the bottom edge, View › Show Filmstrip, or F6."},{"file":"library.html","id":"xmp","title":"XMP sidecars","page":"Organize and find","text":"Ratings, flags, labels, keywords, titles and captions live in the catalog. To let Lightroom, Bridge or Capture One see them, write them to .xmp files next to the originals: Library › Write XMP for Selection (⌘S) or Write All Unsaved XMP (⌥⌘S). Or turn on Settings › Metadata › Automatically write changes into XMP sidecars."},{"file":"library.html","id":"recover-edits","title":"Recover edits, history and snapshots","page":"Organize and find","text":"Writing XMP also saves the current Atelier Develop settings and masks. Develop edits mark the sidecar unsaved; automatic writing includes them. To back up edits made before this feature, select those photos and choose Library › Write XMP for Selection. If you lose the catalog, import the originals with their sidecars into a new catalog. Atelier restores the matching current edits, saved history and snapshots before making previews, even when an import preset is selected. Photos already in the catalog keep their existing edits. RAW and JPEG files with the same name keep separate Develop entries in their shared sidecar; ratings and other standard metadata remain shared. Keep filenames unchanged for recovery. Old metadata-only sidecars still work. Unreadable foreign metadata alone does not block import. Unsupported or damaged Atelier recovery data appears in the import problems; Atelier leaves the sidecar untouched instead of silently replacing your edits. A write that cannot safely preserve existing recovery data also reports a problem. If another, older Atelier version changes current edits while leaving saved history behind, recovery reports the mismatch instead of combining incompatible states. Keep history backups current using this version of Atelier. New sidecar writes include retained History steps, the selected step and redo steps, plus snapshot names, dates and saved looks. Earlier sidecars containing only current edits recover one state, shown as Recovered from XMP. W"},{"file":"library.html","id":"volumes","title":"Drives and the NAS","page":"Organize and find","text":"Atelier remembers photos by drive, not by where it's mounted, so plugging a drive in somewhere else doesn't break anything. When the NAS or a drive is off, its photos stay in the grid from their previews, marked Offline. Library › Refresh Volumes (⌘R) checks again after you connect one. See also: Virtual copies, stacks and removing · Keyboard shortcuts"},{"file":"cull.html","id":"","title":"Cull: pick the keepers","page":"Library","text":"Atelier groups bursts and repeated shots of the same scene, suggests the best of each group, and lets you keep or drop shots with single keys."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"start","title":"Start culling","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Before importingTick Cull before importing in the Import dialog; the button becomes Continue to Culling… and the same dialog moves on to the Cull step. Only keepers go into the import entry. The rest are still cataloged, flagged Rejected, so you can look again later. After importingRight-click an import in the sidebar › Cull…, or Library › Cull Import…. Keepers are flagged Picked and the rest Rejected; nothing is removed."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"view","title":"The Cull view","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"MatchSame FrameSimilar Shots GroupingTight Loose Similar shots · 4 photos ✓ Same frame · 2 photos ✓ Single shots SuggestedKeep Sharpness 100% of the group's best · Face quality — Click to look · Space or double-click keeps or drops · P keep · X drop · arrows moveCancel Import 5 Photos Match and Grouping at the top. Same Frame groups only shots framed alike (someone blinked, moved slightly). Similar Shots (the default) also groups the same scene reframed or zoomed a little. The Grouping slider, from Tight to Loose, regroups instantly. It starts at Tight, so only shots that really match are grouped; slide toward Loose to group more. Groups as rows. Keepers have a check; dropped shots are dimmed. Single shots are listed last and kept. Drag the line between the groups and the large photo to make the list wider or narrower; the photos wrap to fit. The focused photo, large, with its sharpness and, when there are faces, face quality (eyes open, in focus). Atelier marks the best one Suggested and starts it as the keeper. Keys, and Cancel and Import N Photos (or Done when culling an import). Before importing, this line shows the photos being copied to your library while you cull."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"keys","title":"Keys","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"← →Previous or next photo. ↑ ↓The group above or below, at the same place in the row. PKeep. XDrop. Space or double-clickSwitch between keep and drop. ReturnFinish. EscCancel. The keys work as soon as the Cull view opens; there's nothing to click first. The list only scrolls when the photo you move to isn't fully in view, and then just enough to show it. Clicking a photo never scrolls. A group can keep several shots. A RAW+JPEG pair counts as one shot: keeping or dropping it applies to both."},{"file":"cull.html","id":"review","title":"Look at a group again","page":"Cull: pick the keepers","text":"Every photo culled with others shows ◫ 4 (the group's size) in the grid. Select it and choose Photo › Show Cull Group (⇧⌘G), or right-click › Show Cull Group, to see the keeper next to every shot it beat, and change your mind. See also: Import photos · Ratings and flags"},{"file":"photos.html","id":"","title":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","page":"Library","text":"Make several versions of one photo without duplicating the file, see how versions stack, and take photos out of the catalog or move them to the Trash."},{"file":"photos.html","id":"virtual-copies","title":"Virtual copies","page":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","text":"A virtual copy is a second set of edits on the same file: a color and a black and white version, or two different crops. NZ6_9144 ⧉ Copy 1 Make one: right-click › Create Virtual Copy, or Photo › Create Virtual Copy (⌘'). Each selected photo gets one. A copy starts with the photo's current edits, rating, label, keywords and collections (not its flag), then has its own of everything. Editing a copy never changes the original. Copies sit right after the original in the grid with a ⧉ Copy 1 badge. Remove one: right-click › Remove Virtual Copy…. Only the copy's settings go; the file is untouched."},{"file":"photos.html","id":"stacks","title":"Stacks","page":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","text":"Edit In versions, AI results and virtual copies stack on their original: they sort right after it, join the same imports and collections, and the original shows the count (▤ 3)."},{"file":"photos.html","id":"remove","title":"Remove or delete photos","page":"Virtual copies, stacks and removing","text":"Select photos and press Delete, or right-click › Remove Photo… (Remove 3 Photos… with several). The dialog says what will happen, with numbers: how many files, how many virtual copies go with them, how many are on a drive that isn't connected. Choose: RemoveThe default. Takes the photos out of the catalog, with their edits and metadata. The files stay where they are. Delete from DiskAlso moves the files to the Trash. CancelNothing changes (or press Esc). With RAW+JPEG pairs, the dialog has a checkbox per kind of file (for example “RAW (.NEF) 12 files” and “JPEG (.JPG) 12 files”); untick one to keep those files. Nothing is erased silently. Files only go to the Trash. A network share like the NAS has no Trash, so Atelier asks before deleting from it immediately. You'll hear the Dock's poof when something is removed from Atelier, and Finder's Trash sound when files go to the Trash. Turn this off in Settings › General › Feedback. See also: Organize and find · Edit In"},{"file":"develop.html","id":"","title":"Develop basics","page":"Develop","text":"Develop is where you edit a photo. Every change is a setting stored in the catalog, rendered live from the original: the original file is never changed, and every step can be undone. The Develop viewZoom and move aroundNavigator Before and afterPanelsHistory and undo SnapshotsCopy and paste settingsHistogram"},{"file":"develop.html","id":"layout","title":"The Develop view","page":"Develop basics","text":"Select a photo and press D, click Develop in the toolbar, or right-click a photo › Edit in Develop. ImportExport GridLoupeDevelop Navigator Presets › Atelier5› Film6› Black & White6 Snapshots Warm version History Exposure +0.45Profile: Camera MatchingImport Before and After OffSide by SideSlider FitFill100%200% Histogram ▭ ◌ ✧ ⌄ Basic Exposure+0.45Contrast+20Highlights−45 › Tone Curve› HSL / Color› Color Grading› Detail All Photographs · 5 photos · 1 of 5 · NZ6_9144.NEF ⌄ Navigator: the whole photo; shows where you are when zoomed and previews presets. Presets and Snapshots. Click a section's header band to fold it. History: every change you made, newest first. The photo, rendered live. Before and After and the zoom buttons. The tool strip: Crop & Straighten, Masking and AI. The adjustment panels, from Basic to Effects. The Filmstrip: the other photos in the list. Arrow keys move between them."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"zoom","title":"Zoom and move around","page":"Develop basics","text":"Double-click the photo, or ZSwitch between Fit and 100% at the spot you clicked. A single click does nothing, so a stray click never loses your zoom. Fit · Fill · 100% · 200%The buttons under the photo. Fit shows the whole photo; Fill fills the space. Pinch, or ⌥/⌘ + scrollZoom freely, around the pointer. Drag, scroll, or hold Space and dragMove around while zoomed. ⌘= · ⌘- · ⌘0 · ⌥⌘1Zoom in, zoom out, Fit, 100% (View menu). The zoom and the spot you're looking at stay put when you open a tool (Crop, Masking, Guided Upright, the color pickers) and come back. Atelier › Settings… › Develop chooses what a double-click zooms to (50% to 400%) and what happens when you move to another photo: open it at Fit or Fill, return to where you left that photo, or keep the same zoom as in Lightroom."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"navigator","title":"Navigator","page":"Develop basics","text":"The small photo at the top of the left column. When you're zoomed in, a white rectangle shows the part in view. Drag the rectangle to move around the photo. Click anywhere else in the Navigator to jump there. At Fit, a click zooms in on that spot. Rest the pointer on a preset to preview it here, without changing the photo."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"before-after","title":"Before and after","page":"Develop basics","text":"Compare your edit with the original. \\Show the original until you press it again (the Before button under the panels does the same). Side by SideBefore on the left, after on the right. Both zoom and pan together. SliderOne photo with a divider: original on the left, your edit on the right. Drag the divider to compare; drag the photo to move around when zoomed. YStep through Off, Side by Side and Slider. Like this. The Slider view works like this picture: drag across it. A compare view opens at Fit. Going back to Off returns you to the zoom you had."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"panels","title":"Panels","page":"Develop basics","text":"Click a header band to open or fold a panel. Atelier remembers which are open. The left column's sections (Navigator, Presets, Snapshots, History) work the same way. A blue dot next to the name means the panel has edits; ↺ resets just that panel. The on/off switch (every panel but Basic) turns the whole panel off to compare, keeping its settings. While it's off the controls are dimmed but still work: moving one turns the panel back on. Exports and presets follow the switch. Right-click a header for Solo Mode (opening one panel closes the others), Expand All Panels, Collapse All Panels and that panel's Help page. Drag the panel's left edge to make the column wider or narrower."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"group-reset","title":"Reset a group","page":"Develop basics","text":"Double-click a group heading inside a panel (White Balance, Tone, Presence, Region, Sharpening, Grain…) to reset just those sliders. Or hold ⌥: the heading reads “Reset Tone” and one click resets it, as in Lightroom."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"sliders","title":"Sliders","page":"Develop basics","text":"Double-click a slider's name to reset it. Shift-double-click a Tone slider's name to set it automatically. Click the value to type a number; Return sets it, Esc cancels. Bold means changed. Every slider in Atelier works this way, in Masking, Crop and the AI brush too. The small tick on the track is the default; the blue fill is your change. Colored tracks (Temp, Tint, Vibrance, HSL) show which way each end goes. Hold ⌥ while dragging many sliders to see what they affect: see ⌥-drag views."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"color-pickers","title":"Color pickers","page":"Develop basics","text":"The White Balance Selector, Defringe's Fringe Color Selector and Color Grading's color picker all work the same way: click the eyedropper, then click the photo. A hint at the top of the photo says what to click (in orange if the click didn't set anything, with the reason). You can still zoom and move around while it's out, only one is out at a time, and Esc puts it away."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"history","title":"History and undo","page":"Develop basics","text":"Every change is a named step in History (Exposure +0.45, Straighten 4.0°, Preset: Punchy). History is kept with the photo, forever. ⌘Z undoes and ⇧⌘Z redoes, one step at a time. Click a step to go back to it. A new change from there replaces the steps after it. The ✕ in the History header clears the list but keeps the current look."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"snapshots","title":"Snapshots","page":"Develop basics","text":"Save a look you like by name, keep editing, and come back to it. Click + in the Snapshots header and type a name. Click a snapshot to apply it again (that's a history step too). Right-click a snapshot to Update with Current Settings, Rename… or Delete. Two versions side by side in the grid? Use a virtual copy instead: each copy has its own edits."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"copy-paste","title":"Copy, paste and reset","page":"Develop basics","text":"⇧⌘C or CopyCopy this photo's settings. ⇧⌘V or PastePaste them onto the selected photos. Right-click › Develop Settings works on the whole selection. ⇧⌘R or ResetClear every edit on this photo (one undoable step)."},{"file":"develop.html","id":"histogram","title":"Histogram","page":"Develop basics","text":"The graph at the top of the right column shows how much of the photo is at each brightness, from black on the left to white on the right, for red, green and blue. It's drawn from the edited photo and updates as you work. ›Histogram ISO 40050 mmƒ/2.81/250 sec Under the graph: the ISO, focal length, aperture and shutter speed the photo was taken with. Hover over them for the camera, lens and exposure compensation. Clipping triangles. The small triangles in the top corners light up when a color channel reaches pure black (left) or pure white (right), in the color of those channels; white means all three. Click a triangle to see on the photo what is really lost: pure white turns red and pure black turns blue. Saturated colors with only one channel at the end, like a deep blue sky, keep their detail and are not marked. J or Develop › Show Clipping turns both on or off. The colors are only on screen, never in exports. How much is clipped. Hover over a triangle to read the share of the photo it counts. Settings › Develop › Panels can show it next to the triangle as well, from half a percent. Drag to adjust. Hover over the graph and the part under the pointer lights up: Blacks, Shadows, Exposure, Highlights or Whites, from left to right. The line underneath shows that slider and its value. Drag left or right to change it, or double-click to reset it. Next: Basic · Crop & Straighten · Masking"},{"file":"basic.html","id":"","title":"Basic","page":"Develop","text":"The panel you'll use on almost every photo: color or black and white, the starting look, white balance, brightness and contrast, and the presence sliders that add depth and color. TreatmentProfileWhite Balance ExposureContrastHighlights & Shadows Whites & BlacksAutoTexture & Clarity DehazeVibrance & Saturation⌥-drag views ›Basic↺ TreatmentColorBlack & White ProfileCamera Matching White Balance WBAs Shot✎ ToneAuto Presence Treatment: Color or Black & White (V). Profile: the starting look the sliders build on. White Balance: the WB menu, the eyedropper, Temp and Tint. Tone: brightness, contrast and the four range sliders, with Auto. A changed slider shows its change filled in blue from the small mark (the default) and its value in bold. Presence: Texture, Clarity, Dehaze, Vibrance and Saturation. The dot means this panel has edits. Click ↺ to reset just this panel. Basic is the one panel without an on/off switch. Every slider works the same way. Drag the knob, or click the number on the right and type a value. Double-click the slider's name to reset it. Double-click a group heading (Tone, Presence) to reset the group. Click a panel's header to fold it away; right-click a header for Solo Mode, which keeps one panel open at a time."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"treatment","title":"Treatment: Color or Black & White","page":"Basic","text":"Switches the photo between color and black and white. In Black & White, the B&W Mix replaces HSL, so you can choose how bright each color becomes in gray. Press V to switch. Drag across the picture to compare. Color Grading still works in Black & White, so you can tone the gray (Sepia, Selenium)."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"profile","title":"Profile","page":"Basic","text":"The look your photo starts from, under every slider. Only RAW files have a choice; for JPEG and HEIC the menu is dimmed. Camera MatchingThe default. Matches the preview your camera saved in the RAW file, including its Picture Control and Active D-Lighting, so the photo looks like it did on the camera's screen. Apple NeutralApple's plain RAW render: flatter, with more room in the highlights. A good start for heavy edits."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"white-balance","title":"White Balance","page":"Basic","text":"Makes whites white. Fix it first: every color after it depends on it."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"wb-menu","title":"The WB menu","page":"Basic","text":"As ShotWhat the camera chose. AutoAtelier makes the photo's nearly gray colors neutral. Also Develop › Auto White Balance (⇧⌘U). Daylight, Cloudy, Shade, Tungsten, Fluorescent, FlashLightroom's presets for that light (RAW files only). CustomShown once you move Temp or Tint yourself."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"wb-selector","title":"White Balance Selector","page":"Basic","text":"Click the eyedropper next to the WB menu, or press W. The pointer becomes an eyedropper. Click something in the photo that should be neutral gray or white: a cloud, a gray rock, a white shirt. Temp and Tint are set so that spot has no color cast, and the eyedropper goes away. Esc cancels."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"temp-tint","title":"Temp and Tint","page":"Basic","text":"Temp runs from blue to yellow and Tint from green to magenta; the colored tracks show which way is which. For RAW files Temp is in Kelvin (2,000 to 50,000 K). JPEG, HEIC and TIFF files have no real Kelvin, so Temp and Tint go from −100 to +100, with 0 as shot. Try it. Drag the slider. Moving right makes the photo warmer. Shortcut. Shift-double-click the name Temp or Tint for Auto white balance."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"exposure","title":"Exposure","page":"Basic","text":"Overall brightness, in stops: +1.00 is twice as bright. On JPEG, HEIC and TIFF files the brightest tones roll off softly toward white instead of clipping. Try it. Set the brightness of the subject first; fix the brightest and darkest parts with Highlights and Shadows after."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"contrast","title":"Contrast","page":"Basic","text":"Moves bright tones up and dark tones down (or together, when negative). Each color keeps its hue: an orange gets brighter or darker but never turns red or yellow."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"highlights-shadows","title":"Highlights and Shadows","page":"Basic","text":"Recover a bright sky or open up dark areas without touching the rest. They work on whole areas, not single pixels, so a sky or a shadow moves together and its detail stays. Highlights below 0 brings back the bright light rays. Shadows above 0 opens up the dark logs and trunks."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"whites-blacks","title":"Whites and Blacks","page":"Basic","text":"Set the very brightest and darkest points. Whites up and Blacks down give a photo a full range from black to white; watch the histogram's clipping triangles (or press J to see clipping on the photo), or hold ⌥ while dragging (see ⌥-drag views)."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"auto","title":"Auto","page":"Basic","text":"The Auto button next to the Tone heading sets Exposure, Highlights, Shadows, Whites and Blacks from the photo. Contrast is left alone. It's one history step (Auto Tone), so ⌘Z undoes it. Just one slider. Shift-double-click a Tone slider's name to set only that slider automatically."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"texture-clarity","title":"Texture and Clarity","page":"Basic","text":"Both add or soften local contrast, on brightness only, so colors don't shift. Negative values smooth without blurring edges. TextureMedium-sized detail: feathers, skin, bark, foliage. Positive brings it out; negative softens skin. ClarityWider areas, mostly in the midtones: adds punch and depth. Negative gives a soft glow. Texture brings out each feather. Clarity adds depth to the whole scene."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"dehaze","title":"Dehaze","page":"Basic","text":"Removes haze, fog and mist (positive) or adds them (negative). It estimates how much haze lies over each area, so clear dark areas and the sky barely change."},{"file":"basic.html","id":"vibrance-saturation","title":"Vibrance and Saturation","page":"Basic","text":"Saturation makes every color stronger by the same amount. Vibrance boosts muted colors most and leaves already strong ones alone, so it's gentler on skin and less likely to look garish. In Black & White neither has any effect. Before Vibrance +60 Saturation +60"},{"file":"basic.html","id":"option-drag","title":"⌥-drag views: see what a slider does","page":"Basic","text":"Hold ⌥ while you press or drag these sliders. The photo is replaced by a view that shows exactly what the slider is changing, until you let go. Exposure, Highlights, WhitesBlack, with clipped highlights shown in the color of the channel that clips (white where all three clip). Drag until only what should be pure white shows. Shadows, BlacksWhite, with clipped shadows shown (black where all three clip). Detail, Color Grading and Defringe have ⌥ views too; each slider's tooltip says what it shows. See also: Develop basics · Tone Curve · HSL / Color and B&W · Presets"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"","title":"Tone Curve","page":"Develop","text":"Reshape brightness exactly where you want it. The curve maps each tone of the photo (along the bottom) to a new brightness (up the side). Parametric curvePoint curveCommon shapesRGB, Red, Green, BlueRefine SaturationTargeted Adjustment"},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"parametric","title":"Parametric curve","page":"Tone Curve","text":"The quick way to shape tones without placing points. Pick Parametric at the top of the panel. ›Tone Curve↺ ◎ParametricPoint ▲▲▲ Region Targeted Adjustment (◎), then Parametric or Point. The curve over the histogram. Drag up or down on it to move the region under the pointer, which is shaded. Split points: drag a triangle to move where one region ends and the next begins. Right-click the graph to reset regions or splits. HighlightsThe brightest tones, above the right triangle. LightsBetween the middle and right triangles. DarksBetween the left and middle triangles. ShadowsThe darkest tones, below the left triangle. Each goes from −100 to +100. Black and white never move, so Shadows +100 opens up dark areas without turning black gray. Lights up and Darks down is a gentle S-curve. The region curve works on top of Basic tone, as in Lightroom."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"curve","title":"Point curve","page":"Tone Curve","text":"›Tone Curve↺ RGBRedGreenBlue⋯ Input 64 Output 46 Channel: RGB changes brightness; Red, Green and Blue change one color each. The ⋯ menu (also on right-click) has Linear, Medium Contrast and Strong Contrast, your saved curves, Save Curve…, Copy and Paste a channel, Reset, Snap to Grid and Show All Curves. The curve, over the photo's histogram. The dashed diagonal is “no change”. Left is black, right is white. Input and Output of the selected point, 0 to 255. Type a number to move it. Add a point: click anywhere on the curve, then drag it. Up brightens those tones, down darkens them. A curve holds up to 16 points. Move a point: drag it, or with the pointer over the graph press the arrow keys (⇧ for bigger steps). Points stay in order from left to right. Remove a point: drag it off the graph, or double-click it. The two end points always stay."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"shapes","title":"Common shapes","page":"Tone Curve","text":"S-curve (like the one drawn above, or Medium Contrast in the ⋯ menu): darks down, lights up. More contrast in the midtones. Matte: raise the left end point so black becomes dark gray, and lower the right end a little. A faded film look. Basic tone, the region curve and the RGB curve keep each color's hue, as Lightroom does: a curve makes colors brighter or darker, never a different color. The faint second line under the RGB curve shows the combined result of Basic tone and the region curve."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"channels","title":"Red, Green and Blue curves","page":"Tone Curve","text":"These work on one channel at a time, so they do shift color, on purpose. Raise the Blue curve's left end to put blue into the shadows; lower its right end for yellow highlights. Turn on Show All Curves to see the other channels faintly. For tinting by tone, Color Grading is usually easier."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"refine","title":"Refine Saturation","page":"Tone Curve","text":"Shown under the Point curve once the RGB curve is bent. A steep curve makes colors more saturated as well as more contrasty, as in Lightroom (100, the default). Lower it to keep the contrast but hold back the extra saturation; at 0 the curve changes brightness only."},{"file":"tone-curve.html","id":"targeted","title":"Targeted Adjustment","page":"Tone Curve","text":"Click ◎ at the top of the panel, then drag up or down on the photo over the tone you want to change. In Parametric it moves the region sliders for that tone; in Point it adds a point for that tone on the channel shown (or moves the one already there). Each drag is one step in History. Click ◎ again or press Esc to put it away. See also: Contrast · Histogram · Color Grading"},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"","title":"HSL / Color and B&W Mix","page":"Develop","text":"Change one color without touching the others: make greens more golden, calm down orange skin, darken a blue sky. Point Color does the same for one exact color you pick. In Black & White, the same panel decides how bright each color becomes in gray. The eight colorsHueSaturationLuminanceTargeted AdjustmentPoint ColorB&W Mix"},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"bands","title":"The eight colors","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Pick Mixer at the top of the panel. Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Aqua, Blue, Purple and Magenta each have three sliders. Neighboring colors blend smoothly, and grays, whites and the faint color noise in dark areas are never affected. ›HSL / Color↺ MixerPoint Color ◎HueSaturationLuminanceAll Targeted Adjustment (◎), then show Hue, Saturation or Luminance one at a time, or All 24 sliders. Each track is painted with the colors that end of the slider gives, so you can see which way to drag."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"hue","title":"Hue","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Shifts a color toward its neighbors. Green toward yellow gives autumn foliage; blue toward aqua gives tropical water."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"saturation","title":"Saturation","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Makes one color stronger or weaker. Turning down Orange and Red calms skin and wood; turning up Blue deepens a sky. At −100 a color turns gray at the brightness you saw it, so a deep blue sky becomes a dark gray, not a pale one."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"luminance","title":"Luminance","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Makes one color brighter or darker. Darkening Blue makes skies dramatic; lightening Orange brightens faces."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"targeted","title":"Targeted Adjustment","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"Click ◎ next to the channel buttons, then drag up or down on the photo over the color you want to change: the sliders for that color move together (a sky moves Blue and a little Aqua, say). In All, ◎ is a menu: choose Hue, Saturation or Luminance first. Each drag is one step in History; Esc puts the tool away. It works the same way in Tone Curve and the B&W Mix."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"point-color","title":"Point Color","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"For one exact color rather than a whole band: a skin tone, a jacket, the green of one plant. ›HSL / Color↺ MixerPoint Color ✎ Point Color 1 Range ☐ Visualize Range Eyedropper and swatches: click the eyedropper, then the color in the photo. Up to four swatches; click one to edit it, right-click it to delete it. Range widens or narrows which colors count as “this color”. Uniformity pulls them toward the picked color, evening out blotchy skin or a patchy sky (beyond Lightroom, after Capture One's skin tone tool). Visualize Range shows only the colors the swatch changes; the rest of the photo turns gray. HueTurns the picked color toward its neighbors, up to 30°. SaturationMakes it stronger or weaker. LuminanceMakes it brighter or darker."},{"file":"hsl.html","id":"bw-mix","title":"B&W Mix","page":"HSL / Color and B&W","text":"When Treatment is Black & White (press V), this panel shows the B&W Mix instead of HSL: eight sliders that set how light or dark each color becomes in gray, like colored filters on black and white film. B&W Mix at 0 Green +90, Yellow +80, Orange −60 The same conversion with the foliage lifted and the warm wood darkened. Classic filters. Darken Blue for a dramatic sky (a red filter). Lighten Orange and Red for smooth skin. Lighten Green and Yellow for bright foliage. ◎ works here too: drag up or down on a color in the photo to make its gray lighter or darker. Vibrance and Saturation have no effect in Black & White. Color Grading still works, for toned prints (Sepia, Selenium in the Black & White presets). See also: Vibrance and Saturation · Color Grading"},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"","title":"Color Grading","page":"Develop","text":"Tint the shadows, midtones and highlights separately: teal shadows and warm highlights for a film look, a cool cast for night, a sepia tone for black and white. Reading a wheelDragging3-Way and single wheels Blending and BalanceColor pickerCompare and soloRight-click menu Shadows: Hue 195, Saturation 55. Highlights: Hue 35, Saturation 55. Drag across to compare."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"wheel","title":"Reading a wheel","page":"Color Grading","text":"Each wheel is a circle of colors. The angle picks the hue (red at 3 o'clock, then yellow, green, cyan, blue and magenta going counterclockwise) and the distance from the center is how strong the tint is. The center is no tint at all. 1 2 3 4 5 The inner dot (hollow, filled with the tint it gives) sets hue and saturation together. Drag it. The rim dot sets the hue only. Drag it around the edge to change the color but keep the strength. The spoke runs from the center through the dot to the rim, so you can see the direction of the tint. The faint black dots are the colors in your photo that fall in this wheel's range (here, the shadows), like a small vectorscope. A cluster away from the center shows a color cast; as you move the inner dot, the cluster moves with it. The small ring is where another wheel's tint is (here, the warm Highlights tint), so you can line tints up or set them opposite. Beyond Lightroom. The photo's color dots and the other wheels' rings aren't in Lightroom. They update as you edit."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"dragging","title":"Dragging, with modifier keys","page":"Color Grading","text":"Drag the inner dotHue and saturation. It moves from where it is, so it never jumps under the pointer. Click elsewhere on the wheelPut the dot there. Drag along the spokeChanges only the strength: the hue holds until you move well off the line. ⇧ dragSaturation only. ⌘ dragHue only. ⌥ dragFine adjustment: moves at a tenth of the speed. Double-click inside a wheelReset its hue and saturation. Double-click a wheel's nameReset the whole wheel, Luminance included. ← → · ↑ ↓With the pointer over a wheel: turn the hue · change saturation. Add ⇧ for steps of 10."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"views","title":"3-Way and single wheels","page":"Color Grading","text":"The row of buttons at the top of the panel picks the view. Atelier remembers it. ›Color Grading↺ Adjust ⁂ |✎ Midtones 👁 H 0 · S 0 · L 0 Shadows 👁 H 195 · S 55 · L 0 Highlights 👁 H 35 · S 55 · L 0 Blend View buttons: 3-Way, then one large wheel at a time for Shadows, Midtones, Highlights or Global (all tones). Double-click Adjust to reset all Color Grading. Color picker (eyedropper): see Color picker. In 3-Way, each wheel has a Luminance slider under it and its values (H · S · L) below. A single-wheel view has Hue, Saturation and Luminance sliders instead, where you can type exact numbers. Blending and Balance, with a graph of where each range applies: see below. The panel switch turns all Color Grading off to compare, keeping the settings. Every panel but Basic has one. Luminance lightens or darkens that tonal range. It rolls off toward white and black instead of clipping: lightening the shadows lifts and tints the blacks, darkening the highlights tints the whites. Global tints the whole photo, on top of the three ranges."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"blend","title":"Blending and Balance","page":"Color Grading","text":"BlendingHow much the three ranges overlap. Low gives distinct bands; high (the default is 50) blends them smoothly. BalanceMoves the split between shadows and highlights. Negative gives the Shadows wheel more of the photo; positive gives Highlights more. The graph under the sliders shows the three ranges from black (left) to white (right): blue for Shadows, gray for Midtones and yellow for Highlights. It reshapes as you move the sliders. Hold ⌥ on Balance to see the photo painted in the three ranges' colors, so you can see exactly where the split falls. On Blending, ⌥ shows every wheel's tint at full strength. On a single wheel's Hue slider, ⌥ shows that tint at full strength."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"picker","title":"Color picker: remove a color cast","page":"Color Grading","text":"Click the eyedropper at the top right of the panel. The pointer becomes an eyedropper. Click a spot in the photo that should have no color: a gray wall, a white shirt in shadow. Atelier sets a wheel to the opposite tint, so that spot turns neutral. In 3-Way it picks Shadows, Midtones or Highlights by how bright the spot is; in a single-wheel view it sets that wheel. Esc cancels. Beyond Lightroom. Lightroom's Color Grading has no picker. Use White Balance first for an overall cast; use this for a cast in just the shadows or just the highlights."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"compare","title":"Compare and solo","page":"Color Grading","text":"The switch in the panel header turns Color Grading off and on. The settings are kept (and exports, presets and history follow the switch). Press and hold the eye 👁 next to a wheel's name to see the photo graded by that wheel alone."},{"file":"color-grading.html","id":"menu","title":"Right-click menu","page":"Color Grading","text":"Right-click a wheel for Reset Hue and Saturation, Reset Luminance, Reset (the whole wheel), Copy … Settings and Paste Settings to … (copy one wheel's tint to another), and Reset All Color Grading. See also: White Balance · HSL / Color and B&W · Presets (the Film and Cinematic packs use Color Grading)"},{"file":"detail.html","id":"","title":"Detail","page":"Develop","text":"Sharpen fine detail and clean up noise. Judge both at 100%: double-click the photo or press Z first. SharpeningSee what you're sharpeningNoise Reduction ›Detail↺ Sharpening Noise Reduction"},{"file":"detail.html","id":"sharpening","title":"Sharpening","page":"Detail","text":"Amount 0–150How much to sharpen. Start around 40 to 70. Radius 0.5–3.0How wide the sharpened edges are. Low for fine detail (feathers, landscapes), higher for soft subjects. Detail 0–100, default 25Below 25 holds back halos on strong edges; above 25 brings out fine texture. Masking 0–100Keeps sharpening to edges. 0 sharpens everywhere; 100 only the strongest edges, so skies and skin stay smooth. At 100%, sharpening crisps the feathers around the eye and the edge of the beak."},{"file":"detail.html","id":"sharpen-option","title":"See what you're sharpening (⌥)","page":"Detail","text":"Hold ⌥ while you drag a Sharpening slider: AmountThe photo in grayscale, so color doesn't distract you. Radius, DetailOnly the sharpening's edge halos, on mid gray. MaskingA black and white mask: white is sharpened, black is left alone. Drag until flat areas turn black."},{"file":"detail.html","id":"noise","title":"Noise Reduction","page":"Detail","text":"LuminanceSmooths grainy speckle in brightness (high ISO). Too much looks plasticky; stop when the grain is calm. ColorRemoves colored blotches in shadows and flat areas. A little goes a long way. Luminance noise reduction. Color noise reduction. Order. Noise reduction runs before sharpening, so reduce noise first, then sharpen with some Masking so the noise isn't sharpened back. See also: Texture and Clarity · Grain"},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"","title":"Lens Corrections","page":"Develop","text":"Undo what the lens did: bent lines, dark corners and colored edges. Most of it is one checkbox. Remove Chromatic AberrationProfile correctionsDistortionDefringeVignetting ›Lens Corrections↺ Remove Chromatic Aberration Enable Profile Corrections NIKKOR Z 24-70mm f/4 S Distortion Defringe✎ Purple Hue 30/70 Green Hue 40/60 Vignetting"},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"chromatic-aberration","title":"Remove Chromatic Aberration","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Removes red-cyan and blue-yellow edges that grow toward the corners. There are no sliders: Atelier measures how far the red and blue parts of the image are out of line in this photo and lines them up. Turn it on for most photos. A corner of the photo, enlarged: colored outlines on the trunks disappear."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"profile-corrections","title":"Enable Profile Corrections","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Fixes the lens's own distortion and dark corners using the correction data your camera writes into the RAW file (Nikon Z cameras do this). It's on by default for RAW files, and the panel shows the lens's name. When there's no data for a photo (or it isn't a RAW), the checkbox is dimmed with “No lens corrections for this photo”."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"distortion","title":"Distortion","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"For anything the profile doesn't fix, or a lens with no profile. Positive removes barrel distortion (straight lines bowing outward); negative removes pincushion (lines bowing inward)."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"defringe","title":"Defringe","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Removes purple and green fringes along high-contrast edges, mostly from shooting at wide apertures. A fringe pixel loses its color but keeps its brightness. Branches against a bright sky, enlarged 3×. Purple Amount, Green Amount 0–20How much to remove. Higher reaches further from each edge. Purple Hue, Green HueWhich shades count as fringe, on a strip of the colors it covers. Drag an end to widen or narrow the range, or the middle to move it. Keep Green narrow so foliage is safe."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"fringe-selector","title":"Fringe Color Selector","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Zoom in on a fringe (double-click the photo). Click the eyedropper next to the Defringe heading. You can still zoom and move around. Click right on the purple or green fringe. Atelier centers that color's Hue range on it and turns its Amount up to 5 if it was 0. Anything else shows a message and the eyedropper stays out. Esc puts it away. Hold ⌥ while dragging: Amount shows white with only the fringes being removed, in their color; Hue shows black outside the range, so you can see which colors it covers. Only colors in a Hue range that sit near a strong edge change, so purple flowers or a green shirt away from edges keep their color."},{"file":"lens-corrections.html","id":"vignetting","title":"Vignetting","page":"Lens Corrections","text":"Brightens (positive) or darkens the corners of the whole, uncropped photo, to undo the lens's dark corners. Midpoint sets how far in from the corners it reaches. For a creative vignette around a crop, use Effects › Post-Crop Vignetting. See also: Transform (perspective) · Crop & Straighten"},{"file":"transform.html","id":"","title":"Transform","page":"Develop","text":"Straighten leaning buildings, trees and artwork. Upright finds straight lines and fixes the perspective for you; Guided lets you draw the lines yourself. UprightGuidedManual slidersConstrain Crop Tilting the camera up makes vertical lines lean toward each other. Upright makes them parallel again."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"upright","title":"Upright","page":"Transform","text":"›Transform↺ OffAutoLevelVerticalFullGuided Constrain Crop OffNo Upright correction. AutoLike Full, but holds back when the lines are weak, so it never warps a photo hard on thin evidence. A safe first try. LevelMakes horizontal lines level. Rotation only. VerticalAlso makes vertical lines parallel. The usual choice for buildings and trees. FullAlso corrects horizontal perspective, for a wall shot from an angle. GuidedYou draw the lines. See Guided. If there aren't enough straight lines, the button says “Not enough straight lines found” and nothing changes. The correction is saved with the photo; click the mode again to measure again. Upright resets the crop. As in Lightroom, choosing an Upright mode resets the crop's cut and straighten angle and the manual Transform sliders (rotations and flips stay). ⌥-click the button to keep them."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"guided","title":"Guided","page":"Transform","text":"Click Guided or press ⇧T, then drag along edges that should be straight up or straight across. 1 2 3 Drag along a tree trunk or building edge to draw a guide. Each guide counts as vertical or horizontal, whichever it's closer to. The photo straightens once there are two guides, and updates with each guide you add or move (up to four). Two vertical guides fix leaning verticals; add horizontal guides to fix the horizontal perspective too. While you draw or move a guide's end, a 3× loupe beside the pointer shows exactly where you are. Show Loupe or O turns it off. Drag a guide's endMove it. Click a guide, then DeleteRemove it. Clear Guides removes all of them. ⌘⌥ drag the photoMove it with X and Y Offset (add ⇧ to move in one direction only). Esc or DoneClose Guided. A strong correction can push part of the photo out of the frame, guide ends included. Lower Scale to bring it back, or turn on Show Whole Photo to zoom the view out to the whole corrected photo. That changes only the view, never the edit. Beyond Lightroom. Show Whole Photo isn't in Lightroom, and every guide ends exactly straight even with four guides."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"sliders","title":"Manual sliders","page":"Transform","text":"Added on top of Upright, for fine-tuning or for doing it by hand. Vertical −100–100Negative widens the top: fixes buildings shot from below. Horizontal −100–100Turns the photo left or right, for a wall shot from the side. Rotate −10°–10°Small rotations. Aspect −100–100Positive stretches the width, negative the height. Fixes buildings that look too tall after a correction. Scale 50–150%Shrinks or enlarges the corrected photo in its frame. X Offset, Y OffsetMoves the photo sideways or up and down."},{"file":"transform.html","id":"constrain-crop","title":"Constrain Crop","page":"Transform","text":"Perspective corrections leave empty, light gray areas at the edges. Turn on Constrain Crop to crop to the largest rectangle with no gray, keeping your crop's shape. It finds the crop again whenever the correction changes, and the Crop tool won't let the crop leave the photo. Turning it off leaves the crop as it is. See also: Lens Corrections · Crop & Straighten"},{"file":"effects.html","id":"","title":"Effects","page":"Develop","text":"Finishing touches: a vignette that follows your crop, a glow from the highlights, and film grain. Post-Crop VignettingGlowGrain ›Effects↺ Post-Crop Vignetting StyleHighlight Priority ⌄ Glow StyleDiffusion ⌄ Grain TypeStandard ⌄"},{"file":"effects.html","id":"vignette","title":"Post-Crop Vignetting","page":"Effects","text":"StyleHighlight Priority (default) darkens like lower exposure but keeps bright areas bright. Color Priority keeps colors as they are while darkening. Paint Overlay mixes the edges toward black or white: flat and soft. AmountNegative darkens the edges and draws the eye in; positive lightens them for a soft, airy look. MidpointHow far toward the middle it reaches. Lower reaches further in. RoundnessAt 0 the vignette has the crop's proportions. Positive makes it rounder, toward a circle; negative squarer, toward a rounded rectangle. Feather default 50How soft the edge is. 0 gives a hard edge. HighlightsWith a negative Amount in Highlight or Color Priority, brings bright areas in the darkened corners back, such as a lamp or the sun. It's applied to the cropped photo, so it always frames what you kept. To fix a lens's dark corners instead, use Lens Corrections › Vignetting."},{"file":"effects.html","id":"glow","title":"Glow","page":"Effects","text":"Light spilling from the bright parts of the photo. Not in Lightroom Classic (Camera Raw and Luminar have one). StyleDiffusion: a soft, dreamy haze, like a diffusion filter. Bloom: highlights spill light onto what is around them. Halation: the red-orange glow film shows around bright lights, as on CineStill 800T. AmountHow strong the glow is. 0 is off. Range default 50Which tones glow. Higher lets darker tones glow too. Spread default 50How far the light reaches, as a share of the photo's size, so a preview and an export match. WarmthTints the glow warmer (positive) or cooler (negative)."},{"file":"effects.html","id":"grain","title":"Grain","page":"Effects","text":"Film-like grain. It changes brightness, not color, and is strongest in the midtones, so pure black and white stay clean. Type default StandardThe kind of film the grain comes from. Standard is Lightroom-like grain. Fine: the small, tight, crisp grain of slow film such as Ektar or Velvia. Cubic: the larger, sharp-edged, clumpy grain of classic film such as Tri-X, HP5 or Kodak Gold, reaching further into the shadows. Tabular: the smooth, soft, even grain of modern T-grain film such as Portra or T-Max, with cleaner shadows and highlights. Each type is equally strong at the same Amount. The built-in film presets pick the type that fits their film. AmountHow strong the grain is. Size default 25Grain particle size. Above 25 the photo is softened slightly, so large grain replaces fine detail as with real film. Roughness default 500 gives even, regular grain; higher clumps it and varies its strength across the frame. Shown enlarged 2×. Like Lightroom, grain is judged at 100%: at Fit, and in smaller exports, it looks weaker, as it would if the full-size photo were shrunk. See also: Noise Reduction · Film presets"},{"file":"crop.html","id":"","title":"Crop & Straighten","page":"Tools","text":"Trim the edges, change the shape, level a horizon, rotate or flip. Nothing is cut from the original; you can change the crop any time. Open the toolOn the photoThe Crop panelStraighten"},{"file":"crop.html","id":"open","title":"Open the tool","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"In Develop, press R or click the crop button (the first icon under the histogram). Press R, Return or click Done to close it. While it's open you see the whole photo with the crop on top."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"on-photo","title":"On the photo","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"1 2 3 4 Corners: drag to resize from that corner. The pointer turns into a diagonal arrow. Edges: drag to move one side. Inside: drag to move the crop (the pointer is a hand). A rule-of-thirds grid helps you place the subject. Outside: drag to straighten (the pointer is a curved arrow). A finer grid appears while you rotate."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"panel","title":"The Crop panel","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"›Crop & Straighten↺ Drag a corner or edge to crop, inside to move, outside to straighten. AspectOriginal⇄🔒 ↺↻⇋⇵ResetDone Aspect: Original, 1 × 1, 4 × 5 / 8 × 10, 5 × 7, 2 × 3, 3 × 4, 16 × 10, 16 × 9 or Free. The lock keeps the shape while you drag; unlock it to drag freely. ⇄ switches between landscape and portrait (or press X while cropping). Angle: straighten from −45° to 45°. Double-click the name to reset. Rotate left (⌘[), rotate right (⌘]), flip horizontal, flip vertical. Reset removes the crop, straightening, rotations and flips."},{"file":"crop.html","id":"straighten","title":"Straighten","page":"Crop & Straighten","text":"Drag outside the crop, or use the Angle slider. The photo turns smoothly under the frame, and the frame shrinks so it never shows past the photo's edge. Perspective, not just tilt? If verticals lean toward each other, use Transform › Upright instead of rotating. The crop applies everywhere: previews, the grid, exports, Edit In and AI. Masks stay on the same spot of the photo when you change the crop. See also: Transform · Zoom"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"","title":"Masking","page":"Tools","text":"Edit just part of a photo: darken a sky, brighten a face, warm one corner. A mask says where; its sliders say what. Start a maskThe Masks panelLinear GradientRadial Gradient BrushLuminance RangeSelect Subject and ObjectAdd, Subtract, IntersectMask sliders"},{"file":"masking.html","id":"start","title":"Start a mask","page":"Masking","text":"In Develop, click the Masking button (the dashed circle under the histogram) or press ⇧W. Or jump straight to a tool: M Linear Gradient, ⇧M Radial Gradient, K Brush. Click Create New Mask and pick a kind, then draw it on the photo. Move the mask's sliders (Exposure, Temp, Clarity…). They change only the masked area. Press Return or click Done. Masks keep working after Masking closes; open it again any time to change them. You can zoom and move around as usual while masking (Z, double-click, pinch, scroll, or hold Space and drag). Pins and handles stay the same size on screen."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"panel","title":"The Masks panel","page":"Masking","text":"›Masking↺ Mask 1👁 ▤Linear Gradient+ ◐Luminance Range∩ Mask 2👁 New Mask ⌄Add ⌄Subtract ⌄ Show Overlay Mask 1 … Temp, Tint, Contrast, Shadows, Whites, Blacks, Texture, Dehaze, Saturation, Sharpness, Noise Reset SlidersDone Masks, in the order you made them. Click one to select it; double-click to rename. The eye hides its effect. A selected mask lists its parts, each with a badge: + added, − subtracted, ∩ intersected. New Mask, and with a mask selected, Add or Subtract a part: Brush, Linear Gradient, Radial Gradient, Luminance Range, Select Subject, Select Object. Show Overlay (O) tints the selected mask red, so you can see exactly where it lands. The selected mask's sliders."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"linear","title":"Linear Gradient","page":"Masking","text":"A gradual change across the photo, like a graduated filter on a lens: darken a sky, lighten a foreground. With Show Overlay: red is where the change is full, fading to nothing at the bottom line. The result: the bright top darkened, the rest untouched. Draw: drag from where the change should be full to where it should fade out, as in Lightroom. To darken a sky, start at the top and let go at the horizon. Three lines show the start, middle and end. Lines close together give a hard edge; far apart, a soft one. Move: drag the pin in the middle. Widen or narrow one side: drag an outer line anywhere along it; it moves straight, keeping its angle. Turn: drag the dashed center line anywhere along it; the gradient turns around its pin. The pointer shows which one you're on. Move one end freely: drag the round handle on an outer line to move and turn that side at once."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"radial","title":"Radial Gradient","page":"Masking","text":"An ellipse: brighten a face, spotlight a subject, or (inverted) darken everything around it. Drag the pin to move it, the four square handles to resize, the round handle on top to rotate. The dashed inner ellipse shows where the full effect ends. A radial gradient lifts the hiker; the rest is slightly darkened with a second, inverted mask. Draw: drag from the center outward. Feather (0 to 100, default 50) softens the edge. The change is inside by default. Tick Invert to put it outside, for a custom vignette."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"brush","title":"Brush","page":"Masking","text":"Paint the mask where you want it. Drag on the photoPaint. Hold ⌥ while painting, or choose EraseErase. [ · ]Smaller · bigger brush. ⇧[ · ⇧]Less · more feather. Size, Feather and Flow are in the panel. Flow is how much each stroke adds, so low Flow builds up gradually. The pointer is a circle the size of the brush, with an inner circle where the feather begins."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"luminance","title":"Luminance Range","page":"Masking","text":"Selects by brightness: only the parts of the photo between two brightness levels (0 to 100). Smoothness softens the edges of the range. Overlay: the brightest areas Most useful combined. Intersect a Linear Gradient with a Luminance Range to darken only the bright sky, not the trees that reach into it."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"subject","title":"Select Subject and Select Object","page":"Masking","text":"Select Subject finds the main subject (people, animals, things in front) with Apple's Vision, on your Mac. Select Object finds something inside a box you drag, so smaller subjects work too; drag the pin to move the box. Select Subject Both have a Feather slider (default 20) to soften Vision's hard edge. To change the background instead of the subject, tick Invert. If Vision finds nothing, the panel says so."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"combine","title":"Add, Subtract and Intersect","page":"Masking","text":"A mask can have several parts: AddJoins a part to the mask: two radial gradients on two faces. SubtractCuts a part out: brush away the trees from a sky gradient. IntersectKeeps only where both overlap. Right-click the mask › Intersect Mask With. Right-click a mask for Rename, Duplicate, Invert, Intersect Mask With and Delete. Right-click a part for Invert, Switch to Add / Subtract and Delete. Delete removes the selected part (the whole mask if it was the last part)."},{"file":"masking.html","id":"adjust","title":"Mask sliders","page":"Masking","text":"Each mask has its own: Amount (0 to 200%, scales all of the mask's sliders at once), Temp, Tint, Exposure, Contrast, Highlights, Shadows, Whites, Blacks, Texture, Clarity, Dehaze, Saturation, Sharpness and Noise. They work like the Basic sliders. Double-click a name to reset it; Reset Sliders clears them all. Invert shows once, for what is selected: with a part selected it sits with that part's settings and inverts just the part; with the mask row selected it sits under Amount and inverts the whole mask. Temp and Tint go the Lightroom way: more Temp is warmer (yellow), more Tint adds magenta. Change your mind later. Masks stay on the same spot of the photo when you crop, straighten or change the Transform. They're saved with the photo, copied by Copy/Paste Settings and virtual copies, and can be included in presets. See also: History and undo · Create a preset"},{"file":"presets.html","id":"","title":"Presets","page":"Tools","text":"A preset is a saved set of slider values. Apply a look in one click, save your own, and use them on many photos at once. Preview and applyRecentAmountCreate a presetManage presets and groupsBuilt-in packsMany photos at once"},{"file":"presets.html","id":"apply","title":"Preview and apply","page":"Presets","text":"PRESETS＋⇕ Search Presets ⌄ 🕘 Recent2 Chrome 64🔒 Muted Chrome🔒 › User Presets3 › Atelier5 ⌄ Film9 Portrait 400🔒 Gold 200🔒 Vivid Slide🔒 › Black & White8 The Presets section in Develop's left column. ＋ creates a preset; ⇕ collapses every group. Search filters every group by name. Groups: yours first, then the built-in packs. Click a group to open or close it. Built-in presets show a lock. Rest the pointer on a preset to see it on your photo in the Navigator. Click to apply it. Recent: the presets you applied last, newest first. See Recent. Applying is one history step (Preset: Gold 200), so ⌘Z undoes it. A preset only changes the settings it includes; everything else on the photo stays as it was."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"recent","title":"Recent","page":"Presets","text":"The Recent group at the top of the Presets section lists the last six presets you applied, from any group, newest first. Applying one again moves it back to the top. It's there as soon as you've applied a preset, and stays between launches. Right-click a preset in RecentRemove from Recent, then the preset's usual menu. Right-click the Recent groupClear Recent. Right-click photosApply Develop Preset › Recent has the same list. Recent hides while you search (the search already finds every preset). A deleted preset, or one in a hidden pack, drops out of the list."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"amount","title":"Amount","page":"Presets","text":"After you click a preset, an Amount slider appears at the top of the Presets section. Drag it from 0 to 200% to use the look more gently or more strongly. It updates the same history step, and goes away when you edit another setting or open another photo. Double-click it to go back to 100%."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"create","title":"Create a preset","page":"Presets","text":"Edit a photo until you like it. Click ＋ in the Presets header, or Develop › New Preset… (⇧⌘N). Type a Preset Name and pick a Group (or New Group…). Leave it as User Presets if you're not sure. Tick the settings the preset should carry. A setting still at its default shows “(no change)”. Click Create. What's unticked to start. Profile, White Balance, Lens Corrections, Transform, Crop and Masks depend on the photo, so they start unticked, as in Lightroom. The dialog remembers your last choice."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"manage","title":"Manage presets and groups","page":"Presets","text":"Right-click your presetApply · Update with Current Settings… · Rename… · Move to Group · Duplicate · Show in Finder · Delete… Right-click a built-in presetApply · Duplicate to User Presets (to start from it and change it) Right-click your groupCreate Preset in This Group… · Rename Group… · Delete Group… Right-click a built-in packHide Group. Develop › Manage Presets… shows or hides each pack. Photos that used a deleted preset keep their settings. Your presets are kept in one file shared by every catalog: ~/Library/Application Support/Atelier/Presets.json."},{"file":"presets.html","id":"built-in","title":"Built-in packs","page":"Presets","text":"Eight packs, 54 looks, made only from Atelier's own sliders, so they work on RAW, JPEG and HEIC alike. They never touch your exposure, white balance, sharpening, noise reduction, crop or masks, so apply one after you've set those. Each look follows what a film stock or style is known for; the names describe the look, not a brand. No presetThe photo as edited Portrait 400Film · warm skin, celadon greens Pastel 400Film · airy, mint greens Chrome 64Film · deep reds and blues Vivid SlideFilm · contrast and deep color Golden HourLandscape · warm sunset light Muted ChromeStreet · documentary color Teal & OrangeCinematic Dark & MoodyCinematic · deep blacks, muted greens Faded MatteVintage & Matte Classic 400B&W · film grain, yellow filter Dark SkyB&W · deep red filter NoirCinematic · deep B&W, vignette AtelierClean Product, Fine Art & Pigments (true paper whites and pigment vibrance for artwork), Punchy, Warm Film, Black & White FilmPortrait 400 (soft portrait negative), Gold 200 (warm consumer film), Pastel 400 (airy brightness, mint greens, cyan shadows), Vivid 100 (punchy fine-grain negative), Vivid Slide (slide film), Chrome 64 (classic chrome slide), Tungsten 800T (cool cinema film, glowing warm highlights), Expired (faded, color-shifted), Instant Black & WhiteNeutral (a plain conversion to start from), Silver Gelatin (darkroom fiber print), Classic 400 (yellow filter, grain), High Contrast (red filter), Push 400 (hard, coarse grain), Dark Sky (deep red filter for landscapes), "},{"file":"presets.html","id":"many","title":"Many photos at once","page":"Presets","text":"Right-click photos (grid, filmstrip, loupe or Develop) › Apply Develop Preset › a group › a preset. It applies to the whole selection at 100%. While importing: the Import dialog's Apply During Import picks a preset for every new photo. Copy and paste settings between photos: see Develop basics. See also: Match Look · Import photos"},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"","title":"Match Look to a Reference","page":"Tools","text":"Give your photo the color and tone of another picture: a photo you admire, a film still, or one of your own edited shots. The result is ordinary sliders you can keep editing. Open itChoose a referencePreview and StrengthMatch AreasSeveral photos Reference Your photo Matched The cool, quiet palette of the eagle shot carried over to the forest."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"open","title":"Open it","page":"Match Look","text":"In Develop: Photo › Match Look to Reference… (⇧⌘M). Or right-click photos anywhere › Match Look to Reference…, which opens them in Develop first."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"reference","title":"Choose a reference","page":"Match Look","text":"Choose File…Any image on your Mac: JPEG, PNG, HEIC, TIFF or a RAW. Or drag an image from Finder onto the reference box. Recent ReferencesThe last eight references. Only their location and a small thumbnail are kept; a moved file shows as Missing. Use a Photo from the Catalog…Match one of your own shots, with its current edits. Good for making a set consistent."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"preview","title":"Preview, Strength and Show Differences","page":"Match Look","text":"The reference is on the left, a live preview of your photo on the right. Press and hold the preview to see it before the match. Choose what to match: White Balance (Temp and Tint), Tone (Exposure and the Tone Curve), Color (Saturation, HSL and Color Grading). Drag Strength (0 to 100) to blend between your current look and the full match. Click Show Differences to list every slider that will move, from its current value to the new one. Click Apply. Nothing changes on your photo until then; Cancel leaves it exactly as it was. Apply is one history step (Match Look: name). Everything it sets is an ordinary slider, so you can adjust, undo, copy or save it as a preset. No AI touches the pixels."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"areas","title":"Match Areas","page":"Match Look","text":"When the reference shows different things from your photo, turn on Match Areas. A box appears on each image: drag them over the parts that should match (skin to skin, sky to sky) and drag a corner to resize. The boxed areas count most, and the rest of each picture a little, so the whole photo still moves sensibly."},{"file":"match-look.html","id":"several","title":"Several photos","page":"Match Look","text":"With several photos selected in the filmstrip, choose: Same settingsOne match, on the open photo, applied to all. Like Sync. Match each photoEach photo matched on its own, for mixed light. Runs in the background with progress and Cancel in the status bar. See also: Presets · Color Grading"},{"file":"ai.html","id":"","title":"AI tools","page":"Tools","text":"Remove an object, replace a background or enlarge a photo with AI models running on your own Mac. The result is saved as a new photo stacked on the original, which never changes. Set up ComfyUIRemove ObjectReplace BackgroundUpscaleResultsLicenses"},{"file":"ai.html","id":"setup","title":"Set up ComfyUI","page":"AI tools","text":"Atelier sends photos to ComfyUI, a free app you run yourself; nothing is bundled with Atelier. Start ComfyUI (the desktop app, or from Terminal). In Atelier › Settings… › AI, set Server: the ComfyUI desktop app uses http://127.0.0.1:8000, ComfyUI from Terminal uses port 8188. Click Connect. Pick the Edit model and the Upscale model (4×) from what your server has, and a Generation size: 768 px (faster), 1024 px, or 1536 px (slower). The AI panel shows whether ComfyUI is reachable. If it says “ComfyUI not reachable”, start ComfyUI and click Check."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"open","title":"Open the AI panel","page":"AI tools","text":"In Develop, click the wand (the third icon under the histogram), or choose Photo › Remove Object…, Replace Background… or Upscale…. Pick a Recipe, set it up, then click Generate. Progress shows as “Generating… N s” with a bar and a percentage; Cancel stops it."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"remove","title":"Remove Object","page":"AI tools","text":"Choose Remove Object. Paint over what should go. Brush Size (or [ ]), Paint or Erase, and Clear to start again. Optionally say what it is (“a hiker”, “a sign”), which helps the model. Click Generate. Painted Only the area around your strokes is sent, at the generation size, so detail stays high; the result is blended back softly. (Illustration: the real result comes from the model.)"},{"file":"ai.html","id":"background","title":"Replace Background","page":"AI tools","text":"Atelier finds the subject (the background to replace is tinted on the photo), and you describe the new background in words. The subject's pixels are kept exactly."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"upscale","title":"Upscale","page":"AI tools","text":"Enlarge 2× or 4× with an upscale model, for big prints or heavy crops."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"results","title":"Results","page":"AI tools","text":"Each result is a 16-bit TIFF named name-AI Recipe.tif next to the original, stacked on it with a ✦ AI badge in the grid. Atelier records the recipe, models, licenses, prompt and seed with it, so any result can be traced and reproduced."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"licenses","title":"Licenses: what you can publish","page":"AI tools","text":"For your website or shop, use only results from permissively licensed models (Apache 2.0 or MIT), such as Qwen-Image-Edit-2511 (the default for Remove Object and Replace Background) or FLUX.2 [klein] 4B. Non-commercial models such as Qwen-Image-2.1, Fibo 2.0 or FLUX.2 [klein] 9B are for experiments only."},{"file":"ai.html","id":"workflows","title":"Your own workflows","page":"AI tools","text":"Save a ComfyUI workflow in API format as remove-object.json, replace-background.json or upscale.json in the folder from Settings › AI › Show Workflows Folder, and it replaces the built-in one. Title its nodes Atelier Image, Atelier Mask, Atelier Prompt, Atelier Sampler and Atelier Output, as in the example files there. See also: Edit In other apps · Stacks"},{"file":"export.html","id":"","title":"Export","page":"Share","text":"Save finished photos as JPEG, HEIC, TIFF or PNG, with your edits, at the size and color space you need, with your watermark if you want one. Save the choices you use often as presets. Originals are never changed."},{"file":"export.html","id":"open","title":"Open the Export dialog","page":"Export","text":"Select photos and choose File › Export… (⇧⌘E), click Export in the toolbar, or right-click › Export…. In Loupe and Develop it exports the photo you're on (or the filmstrip selection). Export 12 Photos Atelier Website Full-Size JPEG Full-Size TIFF User Presets Shop + Watermark + − FormatJPEG Quality Resize to fit2048pixels Color spacesRGB (web and social) SharpeningFor screen MetadataAll except location Add watermark Save to~/Pictures/Atelier ExportsChange… Show in Finder when done CancelExport 12 Photos Presets fill in everything on the right in one click (see Export presets). The preset that matches the current settings is highlighted; change any setting and the highlight goes, as in Lightroom. Format: JPEG and HEIC (with Quality, 50 to 100), TIFF (16-bit) or PNG. Resize to fit: the long edge in pixels. Off keeps the full size. Color space: sRGB for web and social, Display P3 or Adobe RGB (1998). Sharpening: None or For screen. Metadata: All metadata, All except location (removes GPS), or None. Add watermark puts your name or logo on every photo (see Watermark). Save to a folder. Atelier remembers your last choices."},{"file":"export.html","id":"presets","title":"Export presets","page":"Export","text":"Atelier has three built-in presets: Website (JPEG 85, 2048 px long edge, sRGB, sharpened for screen, no location), Full-Size JPEG (JPEG 95, all metadata) and Full-Size TIFF (16-bit, Adobe RGB, for print or further editing). Hover over a preset to see what it sets. To save your own, set everything up and click + under the list, then name it. A preset keeps the format, quality, size, color space, sharpening, metadata and watermark. It doesn't keep the folder. Right-click a user preset for Update with Current Settings or Delete…. To delete the highlighted preset you can also click −. Built-in presets can't be changed. Using the name of a preset you already have replaces that preset. User presets are shared by every catalog."},{"file":"export.html","id":"watermark","title":"Watermark","page":"Export","text":"Turn on Add watermark to see its settings, with a preview of the first photo above them showing exactly where the watermark will go. StyleText (it starts as © and the year with your name; type anything) in White or Black, or Image: click Choose… and pick a logo. A PNG with a transparent background works best. SizeThe watermark's width as a share of the photo's width. Because it's a share, the watermark looks the same on a 2048 px website JPEG and a full-size TIFF. Opacity100% is solid; lower lets the photo show through. PositionClick one of the nine dots: a corner, an edge or the center. InsetThe gap between the watermark and the photo's edges, as a share of the photo's short side. ShadowA soft drop shadow that keeps white text readable on bright skies. Double-click a slider's name to reset it. The watermark is drawn after resizing and sharpening, so it stays crisp and is never sharpened. If the text is empty or no image is chosen, Export stays dimmed until you fix it or turn the watermark off."},{"file":"export.html","id":"while","title":"While it runs","page":"Export","text":"Exports run in the background, with progress and Cancel in the status bar. Keep working meanwhile. Files keep their original names. An existing file is never replaced: a number is added instead. A virtual copy is named name-Copy1.jpg. Keywords, title, caption and rating are written into the files (unless Metadata is None). Photos whose drive is offline are listed under Show Problems; the rest still export. See also: Edit In other apps · Settings › Export"},{"file":"edit-in.html","id":"","title":"Edit In other apps","page":"Share","text":"Send a photo to Photoshop, Affinity Photo, Pixelmator Pro or any other editor, and get the result back in Atelier, stacked on the original."},{"file":"edit-in.html","id":"how","title":"How it works","page":"Edit In other apps","text":"Select a photo and choose Photo › Edit In › Edit in Photoshop (the first app in the list is ⌥⌘E), or right-click › Edit In. Atelier saves a copy with your edits as name-Edit.tif next to the original (or in Pictures/Atelier/Edits if that folder is read-only) and opens it in the app. The copy appears in the grid right after the original, with a ◪ Edit badge. It joins the same imports and collections and gets the rating, label, title, caption and keywords. Save in the other app. Atelier sees the save and updates the preview. Edit In on a photo that already is an edit opens that file as it is, so you can keep working on it. For the native single-image HDR companion, choose Photo › Edit In › HDR App (Original)…. See Single-image HDR for its original-pixel input, saved project and return workflow."},{"file":"edit-in.html","id":"apps","title":"Choose the apps","page":"Edit In other apps","text":"The menu lists the editors Atelier finds (Photoshop, Affinity Photo, Pixelmator Pro, Photomator, GIMP, Krita). Other Application… adds any app. In Atelier › Settings… › Edit In you can add, rename and remove apps, Find Installed Editors, and choose for each: File16-bit TIFF (best quality) or JPEG. ColorProPhoto RGB (the default, keeps every color), Adobe RGB, Display P3 or sRGB. Include develop adjustmentsOn: the app gets your edited photo. Off: it gets the photo without Atelier's edits. See also: Stacks · AI tools · Export"},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"","title":"Single-image HDR","page":"Share","text":"Shape the available tonal range and texture of one original in Atelier HDR, then finish its color in Atelier Develop."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"start","title":"Open from Atelier","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Select exactly one photo, then choose Photo › Edit In › HDR App (Original)…, or right-click the photo and use Edit In. Atelier creates an HDR project with a verified private source copy. The source file stays unchanged. This input uses original pixels; existing Atelier Develop adjustments are not baked into it. Adjust tone and texture, compare, then click Save & Return to Atelier. The original and returned TIFF appear together in Atelier; the TIFF opens in Develop. This version processes one image. It does not align or merge captured brackets, remove moving subjects, or add scene information by synthesizing exposures."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"controls","title":"Tone and texture","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Tone Mapping Texture Artifacts The first controls focus on tone compression, texture and protection. ExposureGlobal brightness in stops, from −5 to +5. Start here if the original looks dark. RangeCompresses broad lighting differences from 0 to 1, opening dark structure and holding back bright areas while retaining local detail. Higher values create a stronger HDR effect. MicrocontrastRaises or softens medium-scale texture. Watch for halos and noise around high-contrast edges. DetailRaises or softens finer texture. Judge it at 100%. Shadows / HighlightsProtect dark and bright areas from texture enhancement. These are protection amounts, not brightness sliders or noise removal. Double-click a slider name to reset it; click a value to type. Panel switches compare with a group disabled and retain its settings. Moving a control turns its panel back on. A drag is one undo step. Use ⌘Z and ⇧⌘Z for undo and redo. Illustration of tone and texture adjustments on a shared Help photo. Results depend on the original; no clipped sensor detail is invented."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"compare","title":"Compare and zoom","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Choose Off, Side by Side or Split below the photo. Drag the split handle to move the divider. Hold ⌥ for the original. Use the zoom menu for Fit, Fill, 100% or 200%; pinch, double-click, scroll with a zoom modifier, or drag to pan using the same photo controls as Atelier."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"save","title":"Save, reopen and new versions","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"File › Save (⌘S) saves a full-resolution, profiled 16-bit sRGB TIFF and the durable project beside it. Ordinary saves update the same result in Atelier. The saved project retains its source and settings; a TIFF alone cannot reconstruct HDR adjustments. To re-edit, select the returned HDR TIFF in Atelier and choose Photo › Edit In › HDR App (Original)… again. It reopens the associated project. File › Save As New… (⇧⌘S) creates a separate project and result identity, preserving the earlier result. Color grading remains in Atelier Develop."},{"file":"hdr.html","id":"recovery","title":"Cancellation and recovery","page":"Single-image HDR","text":"Cancel (or Esc) stops a save before its next commit boundary and preserves the last committed generation. Closing with unsaved changes asks before discarding them. Reopening validates the owned source and saved generation. A missing or changed source stops with an explanation; click Relink Source… and select a byte-identical backup of the original. The app checks its saved hash before copying it into the project; a different photo is rejected. Interrupted file publication is rebuilt from the committed generation when reopened. Atelier rechecks saved completion receipts after relaunch and rejects mismatched or stale results. Keep the project and its private sources when backing up; the catalog backup alone does not contain them. See also: Edit In · Stacks · Develop basics"},{"file":"settings.html","id":"","title":"Settings","page":"Set up","text":"Open Atelier › Settings… (⌘,). Changes take effect at once. GeneralAppearanceImportDevelopExportEdit InAIMetadataKeyboard"},{"file":"settings.html","id":"general","title":"General","page":"Settings","text":"CatalogWhere the catalog is (Show in Finder) and how many photos it holds. See If the catalog cannot open. BackupsThe catalog is backed up when Atelier quits; choose how many to keep, or Back Up Now. Splash ScreenShow it at launch, Show Splash Folder (add your own images, ideally 1600 × 1000 px or larger; each launch shows the next), and Preview Splash Screen. FeedbackPlay a sound and a puff of smoke when deleting. PreviewsHow much space thumbnails and previews use."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"appearance","title":"Appearance","page":"Settings","text":"Pick a theme from small previews. It changes every window at once. SystemFollows macOS, light or dark. Light · DarkmacOS's own styles. GraphiteCharcoal panels and a dark surround, like Lightroom. DarkroomNear black everywhere, so only the photo stands out. Neutral GrayA mid-gray surround, for judging color and brightness."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"import","title":"Import","page":"Settings","text":"The default for new imports (leave files where they are, or copy into the library folder), the library folder, and Show RAW+JPEG pairs as one photo."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"develop","title":"Develop","page":"Settings","text":"Double-click or Z zooms to50%, 100% (1:1), 200% or 400%. When you move to another photoOpen it at Fit or Fill (the default); return to its last zoom; or keep the same zoom, as in Lightroom. PanelsSolo mode (opening a panel closes the others) and the histogram's clipping warnings."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"export","title":"Export","page":"Settings","text":"The default export folder, and whether to show exported files in Finder. Format, size, color, watermark and presets are in the Export dialog."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"edit-in","title":"Edit In","page":"Settings","text":"The external editors and what each one is sent. See Edit In."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"ai","title":"AI","page":"Settings","text":"The ComfyUI server and Connect, the edit and upscale models, generation size, and the workflows folder. See AI tools."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"metadata","title":"Metadata","page":"Settings","text":"Automatically write changes into XMP sidecars, including current Develop edits, masks, retained history and snapshots for Atelier recovery. Other apps see the standard metadata, such as ratings, keywords and captions. See XMP sidecars."},{"file":"settings.html","id":"keyboard","title":"Keyboard","page":"Settings","text":"Every single-key command (ratings, flags, labels, views, tools) with its key. Click a key and press a new one; a key moves from any command that had it. Delete removes a key, Esc cancels, Restore Defaults goes back to Lightroom's keys. Help › Keyboard Shortcuts (⌘/) lists them all. See also: Keyboard shortcuts"}]};
